Don't let an unset reg_state wipe the one a P-CSCF contact already has.

update_pcontact() assigns _ci->reg_state unconditionally, so any caller that
passes a pcontact_info it did not fill in completely resets the contact to
PCONTACT_ANY. ims_ipsec_pcscf's ipsec_create() is such a caller: fill_contact()
memsets the struct and never sets reg_state, so handling the 401 knocks the
contact save_pending() had just inserted from PCONTACT_REG_PENDING back to
PCONTACT_ANY.

That breaks the 200 OK. update_contacts() looks the contact up with
reg_state = PCONTACT_REGISTERED|PCONTACT_REG_PENDING|PCONTACT_REG_PENDING_AAR,
the state test at the end of get_pcontact_from_cache() can no longer match, and
the whole successful-registration branch is skipped — so the contact is never
moved to PCONTACT_REGISTERED here, a later de-REGISTER cannot find it either,
and, worst of all, ipsec_on_expire is never registered as a usrloc callback.
Nothing then tears the ESP tunnel down when the contact goes away: every ended
registration leaks four SAs, eight policies and a protected port pair out of
ipsec_max_connections.

PCONTACT_ANY is the "unspecified" value everywhere else in this API — it is the
wildcard get_pcontact() searches with — so treat it as "leave alone" on update,
exactly as the expires field immediately below already does. No caller sets it
deliberately: every other update_pcontact() call site in ims_registrar_pcscf
and ims_ipsec_pcscf passes a concrete state.

diff --git a/src/modules/ims_usrloc_pcscf/udomain.c b/src/modules/ims_usrloc_pcscf/udomain.c
--- a/src/modules/ims_usrloc_pcscf/udomain.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_usrloc_pcscf/udomain.c
@@ -352,7 +352,9 @@ int update_pcontact(struct udomain *_d, struct pcontact_info *_ci,
 	ppublic_t *ppublic_ptr;
 	int i;

-	_c->reg_state = _ci->reg_state;
+	if(_ci->reg_state != PCONTACT_ANY) {
+		_c->reg_state = _ci->reg_state;
+	}

 	if(_ci->expires > 0) {
 		_c->expires = _ci->expires;
